[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Do you make your living with a stinger, a MIG gun, or a TIG torch? The clock is officially ticking. I just got back from the biggest welding and automation fair in China, and I\'m telling you guys, I feel like I saw the future. Holy smokes, here\'s all the'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exhibitors. The stuff that\'s going to be coming out in the next year is absolutely going to flip the American fabrication industry upside down. The reality is, your skill of being able to manipulate the puddle and lay down the'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'perfect bead being the ultimate safety career net are quickly ending. I run a metal fabrication shop here in Tennessee every day, and one of my jobs is to see what the latest and greatest tech is coming down the pipeline for us. And'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what\'s coming isn\'t the clunky automation you guys have seen that\'s cost millions of dollars to implement and maybe a week or more to program the machine. It\'s fast, it\'s agile, it\'s cheap, and it\'s coming for all of us.'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This machine right here, uh is this teaching with the button? Can you uh can you show me this? &gt;&gt; Yeah, I can. &gt;&gt; So, pull down to the start. Okay.'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then push button for start. Right here? &gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah, yeah. &gt;&gt; Okay. So, there\'s location one. &gt;&gt; There\'s second point. &gt;&gt; Second point there, so then we move'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here and then way we wait. Again?'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; Okay. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Okay, end point. Press play.'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, how about safety? Press play again. Let me put my hand in the way. Nice. &gt;&gt; Very sensitive.'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yeah, very good. That\'s a big deal with the cobots. Is you guys can be inside the work cell with the machine and you don\'t have to have any safety fences, laser light curtains, none of that stuff cuz the machine is considered a soft robotic. But, they don\'t have UL'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'listing. So, that\'s one of the big challenges here is making sure that they have all the safety certifications so that it\'s legal for us to import them to the US cuz you can find the cool stuff, but we can\'t bring it in then'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'no dice. Ooh, I see another one over here. Maybe they have the right certifications. So, you guys see the robots are getting pretty user-friendly and I\'m super impressed with the way that you teach that cobot how to weld. But, that brings'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up a whole other problem. When importing machines directly, it\'s the wild west and really anything can happen. But, running a fabrication shop has taught me quite a few things. One of which is how to spot somebody building quality parts'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or somebody that\'s cutting every corner just to make a buck and use me in the process because it\'s not just about wasting money. Some of this stuff can also cost you your eyesight or worse. So, check this out on the show floor'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where this is one of the classic scams you\'ll find where somebody\'s trying to sell me a laser welding hood. Yeah, no safety fence here, boys. You can just walk right up to this bad mother. They\'d absolutely never allow this in the United States. You\'d have to have like safety fences, guardrails, all kinds of'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'crap here, but no, they don\'t care. Just step right up and see it. You get bit, your finger, your fault. This for laser welding? &gt;&gt; Uh yes. &gt;&gt; Let\'s go across to the other booth and'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see if it auto darkens. Uh bigger bigger viewing window. Yeah. Yeah, that one\'s nicer. Nice big window on it. &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; So, this is uh &gt;&gt; ADF.'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; 1,080 nanometers? &gt;&gt; Uh yeah. &gt;&gt; What OD? OD7? OD8? &gt;&gt; OD7? OD8? &gt;&gt; Yeah, the rating on this. &gt;&gt; Uh yes. &gt;&gt; What is? Can you ask her?'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Got to make sure they got the right certification on these two, that\'s for damn sure. So, for like a 2000 W laser welder, OD6 is plenty. Um, OD7 plus is, you know, recommended. Just give yourself a little leeway cuz'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it is made in China. And uh, ultimately we\'re looking for an OD8 cuz then you have the highest level protection. And OD8 is going to be good for like a 12 kW laser, which you\'re never going to have'

[04:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh, in a laser welder. So, um, it\'ll be a little overkill, but you\'ll be happy when you can see at the end of the day. Okay, on further inspection, they do not have uh, laser welding helmets. They just tried to tell'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'me they did. So, um, we got their catalog, we dug in, there\'s no OD8, there\'s no wavelength certification. So, they\'re just selling regular welding helmets and telling people that they\'re laser welding helmets, apparently.'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I am way out of here. I got to find a trash can for this thing. Oh, I see another cobot. Let\'s see if it\'s got a good UI. I don\'t see a welder on it, just a big tank. Maybe someday, guys.'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
