[10:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'both okay. &gt;&gt; Okay. &gt;&gt; No no model. You have model. &gt;&gt; Oh, okay. &gt;&gt; This one is we can import the model. It\'s a robot we will do the welding by itself.'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yes. &gt;&gt; Another way we you can use the without the model. &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; the camera will rebuild it on the computer. &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; And then we will found the sound uh the welding thing and do do'

[11:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to do the welding. &gt;&gt; So, you can either load in the CAD model and then it\'ll scan and make sure that it\'s in the right position or it can scan itself and create its own STP file and then you'

[11:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just sit there at the workstation and assign where you excuse me then you assign where you want it to weld and what type of weld you want to stitch weld, fillet weld, butt weld, um how wide you want it, then you can'

[11:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of tweak in on what angle you want that gun to sit at. It\'s getting wild out here guys. That would be the ultimate gap filler for the cobots in shop. You just tack a part together, machine scans it for you'

[11:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then you just tell it where you want it to weld, let it roll and go do something else in the shop. Then you feed that video into AI and train it on [music] what a good weld is and a bad weld and pretty soon the machine can fix its'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'own parameters. Oh, it\'s already doing it guys. That\'s the computer vision picking up the characteristics of the weld puddle. It\'s not doing very good job but it\'s only going to get better. Here we go. That looks like a nice'

[12:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'weave. [music] Oh dang yeah, it\'s even tracking is that the slag shelf behind it in the green? Yeah, we can demo the camera. Okay, tell us what it is. Nice.'

[12:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, let\'s get him to cut a sample on this. Here\'s your little resonator, DXF file loaded in. Good quality too, honestly.'

[13:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s a nice edge. So, I want to shoot straight with you guys and share all this info with you not to freak you out but let you know what\'s coming and let\'s talk about what are the AI proof jobs and roles that are going to be available in the shop in the'

[13:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next 5 to 10 years because I see hand welding is going to turn into a very small niche in the market here a A like stick welding. People are going to be talking about MIG just like they\'re talking about stick today. Like yes, it'

[13:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'works. It\'s good for some situations, but it\'s never the best thing you can use. Just a ton of computer vision stuff going on over here. Trying to fill that skills gap. Scanning on the rotary. It\'s coming for us guys. Be ready.'

[13:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Automation is going to be the same story. So, the one big humongous gap that I saw in all this tech is the fit up. So, if you are simply a welder on the line right now, it\'s time to start getting fabrication skills. Fabrication'

[13:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is still a huge gap for the robots to handle because they can\'t do the meticulous layout, run the saw, fit the parts up, make sure everything\'s square, plumb, aligned, accurately measured, and'

[14:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tack the thing together. Every single setup I saw had a pre-tacked and fit up part that then was ready for the welding automation to take over. So, if you guys are not learning fabrication skills'

[14:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right now, if you just went to a welding school, and you think that\'s enough, it\'s not going to be enough. You guys have to level up and find these other skills in the shop that the robots and AI can\'t replace because the future is changing quickly. I mean, just what'

[14:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'ve seen in the last 5 years with laser welding processes taking over tons of different segments of the fab shop, we\'re going to see the same with welding automation and robots and cobots in our shops. The other place that really has a'

[14:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lot of opportunity is in the design. So, if you are a welder and you learn how to draft and use a CAD program like Fusion 360, SolidWorks, Tekla, and you have the fabrication skills, you will be a much'

[15:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'better designer, and you will be able to run circles around any engineer that walks into the shop that knows how to use CAD but\'s never put anything together in real life because these robots are not going to be perfect. You\'re still going to have to know how'

[15:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to fit stuff up, how weld shrink, which order you can weld stuff in to keep something from getting racked or out of plumb. And the final thing that I think is a great opportunity for all of you is if you get into sales. Sales is always'

[15:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to be a human-to-human connection. A robot will never be able to walk into a room, shake hands, and build a connection with people. I have massive loyalty to all of the people that I\'ve come into contact with. I actually'

[15:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'follow project managers, sales people, superintendents, and VPs to different companies because I have more faith in them as a person than I do in the company that they were representing. So,'

[15:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'become the person that people rely on. Become the person that they make a connection with, and you will always have a spot in the fab shop. And speaking of valuable tech, I was actually in China to do a QC check on'

[16:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some machines and also to find the best laser welder in the world. So, I\'m really excited to share with you guys about what\'s going on in the laser welding front. We\'ve got two of the best machines coming in to do a head-to-head'

[16:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'competition here in our fab shop. We\'re going to torture test these things and see how they hold up in real-world conditions. Dang, this thing\'s light. Okay. Temperature control?'

[16:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; Can you uh change temperature from the gun or just tells you what temperature is? &gt;&gt; tell you what the temperature is. &gt;&gt; Okay. Do you have any uh laser welding guns'

[16:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that will uh retract and extend the wire from the gun? &gt;&gt; The wire. &gt;&gt; Yes, but at the gun? &gt;&gt; No. &gt;&gt; No, okay. Oh, wire feeder separate?'

[16:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; Yeah, wire feeder separate unit. All right, once again ran into a safety issue with that other laser welder company. They don\'t have the FDA certification for export to the US, and they don\'t have the integrated wire'

[17:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feeder. They do have up to a four-wire feeder as like a separate box, which I think is pretty cool cuz right now um my favorite supplier has a double wire feeder where the first wire feeder is built into the unit, and then the second'

[17:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wire feeder is like a separate box. I think the double wire feeder also has a place in the shop if your fit up isn\'t, you know, 16th or less. It can really come in handy. You won\'t have to agitate the puddle, do any whipping, pulsing,'

[17:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'throttling button with your hand like you would with a MIG to fill a gap. You can just run that sucker down. Oh, they got me with the color. I like the yellow. Straight from the gun. Oh, what you want?'

